{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014k8P9rRJzo1cpksLVrCh4j","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Memory — xling_calib_direction\\\\n\\\\n## 环境配置\\\\n- **服务器**: westd-13176, 4× RTX 6000 PRO 96GB\\\\n- **环境**: conda base (`source /root/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate base`)\\\\n- **模型路径**: `/root/autodl-tmp/models/Qwen/Qwen2___5-7B-Instruct`（symlink `/root/autodl-tmp/models/Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`）\\\\n- **代码路径**: `/root/xling_calib_direction/`\\\\n- **Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct**: 28 layers (index 0-27), hidden_dim=3584\\\\n- **网络**: 不要用 `source /etc/network_turbo`（会破坏 HF SSL），用 `HF_ENDPOINT=https://hf-mirror.com`\\\\n- **模型下载**: 用 ModelScope（AutoDL 上 HF 代理太慢）\\\\n\\\\n## 数据集字段差异\\\\n- cais/mmlu: `choices` (list), `answer` (int 0-3)\\\\n- CohereForAI/Global-MMLU: `option_a/b/c/d` (str), `answer` (letter \\\\\\\"A\\\\\\\"-\\\\\\\"D\\\\\\\")\\\\n- `normalize_row()` 已处理两种格式\\\\n\\\\n## 激活提取速度\\\\n- Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, BF16, RTX 6000 PRO 96GB: ~1 sample/s (MMLU 5-shot)\\\\n- 14K samples × 7 layers × 6 语言 ≈ 2h per GPU\\\\n\\\\n## MVP 实验关键发现 (2026-05-20)\\\\n\\\\n### Mean-shift alignment 完全无效（方法论发现）\\\\nMean-shift 对所有样本加同一常数向量 `(mean_en - mean_tgt)`。\\\\n- **不能改变 AUROC**: 经线性 probe 后等价于 logit 加常数，AUROC（rank-based）不变。\\\\n- **不能改善 calibration**: ΔECE 测试结果 0/5 语言达标。zh/ar 仅 marginal（<0.4pp），hi/sw 反而大幅恶化（+2.2pp/+6.4pp）。\\\\n- **结论**: 简单常数平移不足以 align 跨语言 representation。需要非常数方法（CDA/CORAL/Procrustes）。这是论文贡献空间。\\\\n\\\\n### Temperature scaling 有效修复 ECE\\\\n- Layer 24: T=5.36, ECE 0.1982→0.0888\\\\n- Layer 27: T=6.21, ECE 0.1951→0.0744\\\\n- T>1 说明 LogisticRegression 过于自信（overconfident），需要 softening\\\\n\\\\n### Layer 27 vs Layer 24: 最后一层更 language-specific\\\\n- EN AUROC: L27=0.7541 > L24=0.7486（最后一层英语 probe 最强）\\\\n- 但跨语言迁移: L24 全面优于 L27（zh 0.686 vs 0.661, ar 0.651 vs 0.635, hi 0.652 vs 0.626, ko 0.648 vs 0.607）\\\\n- **解读**: 最后一层 representation 更 language-specific，对 zero-shot transfer 不利。Optimal transfer layer ≠ optimal source-language layer。这是论文可讨论的有趣发现。\\\\n\\\\n### Swahili 始终最差\\\\n- Best zero-shot AUROC: sw=0.560 (L27) / 0.544 (L20)，均 < 0.58 阈值\\\\n- 与 low-resource 假设一致，论文中作为 expected limitation\\\\n\\\\n### 最终 Gate Check (2026-05-20)\\\\n- P1 ✅ PASS (AUROC=0.7541, ECE=0.0744 with temp scaling)\\\\n- P2 ✅ PASS (4/5 langs)\\\\n- P3 ✅ PASS (14.28pp)\\\\n- P4 ❌ FAIL (mean-shift 无效，0/5)\\\\n- 无 hard fail\\\\n- Overall: PARTIAL 3/4, 核心假设获强支持\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 2071, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DLimNe6JgekSJoEQPJx93R","session_id":"8b4c3866-7c97-4fea-98c8-a02346ba8153","uuid":"f0ab0b55-a467-4088-9a53-26234c97169c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:52:28.769Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PkMb4LzVqWc4WvbzczZbcu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HdvoCpwWqkAhpqTaQVKp96","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in an academic paper review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\nThe paper is: \"Does Probe-Based Calibration Work Beyond English? A Systematic Study Across 3 Architectures and 52 Languages\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper studies whether English-trained correctness probes (linear probes on internal LLM representations predicting answer correctness) transfer cross-lingually. It evaluates 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense), 20-52 languages, 5 transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot 25/100, CaCLR), on GlobalMMLU, Belebele, MMLU-ProX.\n\n## Key Claims and Findings\n1. **Transfer largely fails**: Zero-shot transfer succeeds for only 7/19 languages on Qwen3 (AUROC≥0.65), clear resource-tier gradient\n2. **Pareto frontier is architecture-dependent**: ρ=0.41 Qwen3, 0.22 Llama4, 0.48 Gemma4; cross-architecture agreement τ=0.33\n3. **CaCLR negative result**: Novel consistency-based objective (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency). Consistency loss is inert — β sweeps 3 OOM with <0.3pp change. Removing it improves by 0.2pp. Linear projection matches or beats CaCLR.\n4. **Difficulty-capability entanglement**: Projecting out difficulty hurts transfer 1.3-1.4pp. Contradicts clean separation in English-only prior work.\n5. **Orthogonality persists universally**: Correctness-confidence cosine 0.072 mean, all <0.15. Extends Miao & Ungar [2026] English-only finding to 20 languages. Resource-tier gradient (high: 0.054, low: 0.098).\n6. Extended to 52 languages: pass rate 23.5%, very-low 0%\n7. Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier than MoE\n\n## Related Work as Cited\n- Burns et al. 2023 (CCS/truth directions)\n- Miao et al. 2024 (correctness probes, cross-task transfer, English-only)\n- Miao & Ungar 2026 (orthogonality, representation steering, English-only)\n- Xiao et al. 2025 (knowledge-boundary probes, 8 languages — answerability not correctness)\n- Civelli et al. 2024 (IRT difficulty transfer)\n- Zhou et al. 2024 LACE (per-language layer ensembles, needs target labels)\n- Huang et al. 2024 MKA (multilingual knowledge augmentation, 5× cost)\n\n## Paper Framing\nThe paper frames itself as a \"field-level diagnostic\" — the contribution is the systematic answer to whether probe-based calibration generalizes cross-lingually, regardless of whether results are positive or negative. It targets EACL-level venue (per narrative guidance, though paper itself doesn't state this). CaCLR is presented as both a methodological contribution and a negative result.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the contribution genuinely novel? Is \"diagnostic study with negative results\" sufficient for a top venue?\n2. **Related work completeness**: What's missing? Are connections to existing literature adequate?\n3. **Framing and positioning**: Is the \"diagnostic paper\" framing convincing? Is the dual CaCLR framing (contribution + negative result) coherent?\n4. **Incremental vs substantial**: Which findings are genuinely new vs. predictable extensions?\n5. **Missing theoretical grounding**: Does the paper explain WHY transfer fails at a deep level?\n6. **Impact**: Would this change how the community thinks about probe-based calibration?\n\nConsider these potentially missing related work areas:\n- Cross-lingual representation alignment (MUSE, cross-lingual word embeddings literature)\n- Probing methodology critiques (Hewitt & Liang 2019, Voita & Titov 2020)\n- Representation similarity analysis (CKA, SVCCA) for cross-lingual settings\n- Conformal prediction and other calibration alternatives\n- Domain adaptation theory (Ben-David et al.) that could explain transfer failure\n\nScore on 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nBe thorough and critical. Output your review in structured format with Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions, Minor Issues, and Score with justification. Keep under 800 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45466,"cache_read_input_tokens":23203,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":45466},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b4c3866-7c97-4fea-98c8-a02346ba8153","uuid":"0cba1a83-daa2-42ba-a8a7-bc7c6508e39a"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aa6083b7427c9e84e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HdvoCpwWqkAhpqTaQVKp96","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in an academic paper review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\nThe paper is: \"Does Probe-Based Calibration Work Beyond English? A Systematic Study Across 3 Architectures and 52 Languages\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper studies whether English-trained correctness probes (linear probes on internal LLM representations predicting answer correctness) transfer cross-lingually. It evaluates 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense), 20-52 languages, 5 transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot 25/100, CaCLR), on GlobalMMLU, Belebele, MMLU-ProX.\n\n## Key Claims and Findings\n1. **Transfer largely fails**: Zero-shot transfer succeeds for only 7/19 languages on Qwen3 (AUROC≥0.65), clear resource-tier gradient\n2. **Pareto frontier is architecture-dependent**: ρ=0.41 Qwen3, 0.22 Llama4, 0.48 Gemma4; cross-architecture agreement τ=0.33\n3. **CaCLR negative result**: Novel consistency-based objective (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency). Consistency loss is inert — β sweeps 3 OOM with <0.3pp change. Removing it improves by 0.2pp. Linear projection matches or beats CaCLR.\n4. **Difficulty-capability entanglement**: Projecting out difficulty hurts transfer 1.3-1.4pp. Contradicts clean separation in English-only prior work.\n5. **Orthogonality persists universally**: Correctness-confidence cosine 0.072 mean, all <0.15. Extends Miao & Ungar [2026] English-only finding to 20 languages. Resource-tier gradient (high: 0.054, low: 0.098).\n6. Extended to 52 languages: pass rate 23.5%, very-low 0%\n7. Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier than MoE\n\n## Related Work as Cited\n- Burns et al. 2023 (CCS/truth directions)\n- Miao et al. 2024 (correctness probes, cross-task transfer, English-only)\n- Miao & Ungar 2026 (orthogonality, representation steering, English-only)\n- Xiao et al. 2025 (knowledge-boundary probes, 8 languages — answerability not correctness)\n- Civelli et al. 2024 (IRT difficulty transfer)\n- Zhou et al. 2024 LACE (per-language layer ensembles, needs target labels)\n- Huang et al. 2024 MKA (multilingual knowledge augmentation, 5× cost)\n\n## Paper Framing\nThe paper frames itself as a \"field-level diagnostic\" — the contribution is the systematic answer to whether probe-based calibration generalizes cross-lingually, regardless of whether results are positive or negative. It targets EACL-level venue (per narrative guidance, though paper itself doesn't state this). CaCLR is presented as both a methodological contribution and a negative result.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the contribution genuinely novel? Is \"diagnostic study with negative results\" sufficient for a top venue?\n2. **Related work completeness**: What's missing? Are connections to existing literature adequate?\n3. **Framing and positioning**: Is the \"diagnostic paper\" framing convincing? Is the dual CaCLR framing (contribution + negative result) coherent?\n4. **Incremental vs substantial**: Which findings are genuinely new vs. predictable extensions?\n5. **Missing theoretical grounding**: Does the paper explain WHY transfer fails at a deep level?\n6. **Impact**: Would this change how the community thinks about probe-based calibration?\n\nConsider these potentially missing related work areas:\n- Cross-lingual representation alignment (MUSE, cross-lingual word embeddings literature)\n- Probing methodology critiques (Hewitt & Liang 2019, Voita & Titov 2020)\n- Representation similarity analysis (CKA, SVCCA) for cross-lingual settings\n- Conformal prediction and other calibration alternatives\n- Domain adaptation theory (Ben-David et al.) that could explain transfer failure\n\nScore on 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nBe thorough and critical. Output your review in structured format with Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions, Minor Issues, and Score with justification. Keep under 800 words.","uuid":"d09e78b2-687f-4e71-8a65-2ec02ac49d5b","session_id":"8b4c3866-7c97-4fea-98c8-a02346ba8153"}
