{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HdvoCpwWqkAhpqTaQVKp96","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Score: **3 (Weak Reject)**.\n\nKey concerns:\n1. **Predictable negative result** — transfer failure for low-resource languages follows directly from domain adaptation theory (Ben-David et al.), which the paper neither cites nor engages with.\n2. **Incoherent CaCLR framing** — the method's distinguishing component (consistency loss) is experimentally inert, making the \"method contribution\" claim hollow.\n3. **Missing related work** — cross-lingual alignment (MUSE/VecMap), probing critiques (Hewitt & Liang 2019), representation similarity (CKA), and conformal prediction are all absent.\n4. **Descriptive without mechanistic depth** — documents failure without explaining why at the representation level.\n\nThe paper could strengthen to borderline accept by: (a) adding CKA analysis explaining the Pareto frontier mechanistically, (b) reframing CaCLR honestly as a negative result rather than a contribution, (c) engaging with domain adaptation theory to derive predictions, and (d) comparing against conformal prediction baselines."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa6083b7427c9e84e (use SendMessage with to: 'aa6083b7427c9e84e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 28242\ntool_uses: 9\nduration_ms: 104587</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b4c3866-7c97-4fea-98c8-a02346ba8153","uuid":"b1a5da67-c7fe-4e62-b2c5-6581dd39ed83","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:54:15.448Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in an academic paper review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\nThe paper is: \"Does Probe-Based Calibration Work Beyond English? A Systematic Study Across 3 Architectures and 52 Languages\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper studies whether English-trained correctness probes (linear probes on internal LLM representations predicting answer correctness) transfer cross-lingually. It evaluates 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense), 20-52 languages, 5 transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot 25/100, CaCLR), on GlobalMMLU, Belebele, MMLU-ProX.\n\n## Key Claims and Findings\n1. **Transfer largely fails**: Zero-shot transfer succeeds for only 7/19 languages on Qwen3 (AUROC≥0.65), clear resource-tier gradient\n2. **Pareto frontier is architecture-dependent**: ρ=0.41 Qwen3, 0.22 Llama4, 0.48 Gemma4; cross-architecture agreement τ=0.33\n3. **CaCLR negative result**: Novel consistency-based objective (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency). Consistency loss is inert — β sweeps 3 OOM with <0.3pp change. Removing it improves by 0.2pp. Linear projection matches or beats CaCLR.\n4. **Difficulty-capability entanglement**: Projecting out difficulty hurts transfer 1.3-1.4pp. Contradicts clean separation in English-only prior work.\n5. **Orthogonality persists universally**: Correctness-confidence cosine 0.072 mean, all <0.15. Extends Miao & Ungar [2026] English-only finding to 20 languages. Resource-tier gradient (high: 0.054, low: 0.098).\n6. Extended to 52 languages: pass rate 23.5%, very-low 0%\n7. Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier than MoE\n\n## Related Work as Cited\n- Burns et al. 2023 (CCS/truth directions)\n- Miao et al. 2024 (correctness probes, cross-task transfer, English-only)\n- Miao & Ungar 2026 (orthogonality, representation steering, English-only)\n- Xiao et al. 2025 (knowledge-boundary probes, 8 languages — answerability not correctness)\n- Civelli et al. 2024 (IRT difficulty transfer)\n- Zhou et al. 2024 LACE (per-language layer ensembles, needs target labels)\n- Huang et al. 2024 MKA (multilingual knowledge augmentation, 5× cost)\n\n## Paper Framing\nThe paper frames itself as a \"field-level diagnostic\" — the contribution is the systematic answer to whether probe-based calibration generalizes cross-lingually, regardless of whether results are positive or negative. It targets EACL-level venue (per narrative guidance, though paper itself doesn't state this). CaCLR is presented as both a methodological contribution and a negative result.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the contribution genuinely novel? Is \"diagnostic study with negative results\" sufficient for a top venue?\n2. **Related work completeness**: What's missing? Are connections to existing literature adequate?\n3. **Framing and positioning**: Is the \"diagnostic paper\" framing convincing? Is the dual CaCLR framing (contribution + negative result) coherent?\n4. **Incremental vs substantial**: Which findings are genuinely new vs. predictable extensions?\n5. **Missing theoretical grounding**: Does the paper explain WHY transfer fails at a deep level?\n6. **Impact**: Would this change how the community thinks about probe-based calibration?\n\nConsider these potentially missing related work areas:\n- Cross-lingual representation alignment (MUSE, cross-lingual word embeddings literature)\n- Probing methodology critiques (Hewitt & Liang 2019, Voita & Titov 2020)\n- Representation similarity analysis (CKA, SVCCA) for cross-lingual settings\n- Conformal prediction and other calibration alternatives\n- Domain adaptation theory (Ben-David et al.) that could explain transfer failure\n\nScore on 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nBe thorough and critical. Output your review in structured format with Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions, Minor Issues, and Score with justification. Keep under 800 words.","agentId":"aa6083b7427c9e84e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Score: **3 (Weak Reject)**.\n\nKey concerns:\n1. **Predictable negative result** — transfer failure for low-resource languages follows directly from domain adaptation theory (Ben-David et al.), which the paper neither cites nor engages with.\n2. **Incoherent CaCLR framing** — the method's distinguishing component (consistency loss) is experimentally inert, making the \"method contribution\" claim hollow.\n3. **Missing related work** — cross-lingual alignment (MUSE/VecMap), probing critiques (Hewitt & Liang 2019), representation similarity (CKA), and conformal prediction are all absent.\n4. **Descriptive without mechanistic depth** — documents failure without explaining why at the representation level.\n\nThe paper could strengthen to borderline accept by: (a) adding CKA analysis explaining the Pareto frontier mechanistically, (b) reframing CaCLR honestly as a negative result rather than a contribution, (c) engaging with domain adaptation theory to derive predictions, and (d) comparing against conformal prediction baselines."}],"totalDurationMs":104587,"totalTokens":28242,"totalToolUseCount":9,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1952,"cache_read_input_tokens":26034,"output_tokens":255,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1952},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":255,"cache_read_input_tokens":26034,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1952,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1952,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":1,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a8feaccbaabb0c33d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01DLimNe6JgekSJoEQPJx93R","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":75360,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":126939},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"20427e12-4011-4bc6-8ac4-fdd46e424b63","session_id":"8b4c3866-7c97-4fea-98c8-a02346ba8153"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FEdchHv2ZxWW1CQtrXtTQE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W6bEjMCMd8f1MuANXqDtM3","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"xling_calib_direction","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T185641","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4/6)","report":"## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper systematically evaluates whether English-trained correctness probes on internal LLM representations transfer cross-lingually, across 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B, Llama 4 Scout, Gemma 4 31B), 20-52 languages, and 5 transfer conditions. It discovers a depth-transferability Pareto frontier, proposes CaCLR (a joint calibration-consistency objective), performs difficulty-capability decomposition, and extends the correctness-confidence orthogonality finding to multilingual settings.\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Ambitious and well-structured experimental scope.** 10 experiment plans covering 3 architectures, up to 52 languages, multiple benchmarks, and systematic ablations. The causal chain (discovery → analysis → method → mechanism) is logically coherent. The coverage of MoE vs Dense architectures strengthens generalizability claims.\n\n**S2. Principled negative results honestly reported.** The consistency loss being inert (sweeping beta over 3 OOM changes AUROC <0.3pp), mean-shift failing entirely, and CaCLR never beating linear projection on cross-benchmark evaluation are all valuable findings for the community. The paper does not oversell.\n\n**S3. Comprehensive baseline coverage.** Five transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot 25/100, CaCLR) provide a fair comparison landscape. The INT8 validation ablation (cosine 0.994, 0.3pp AUROC difference) proactively addresses quantization concerns.\n\n**S4. The difficulty-capability decomposition (Plan 004) is methodologically interesting.** Projecting out IRT difficulty and showing this *hurts* transfer by 1.3-1.4pp is a counterintuitive and informative result that challenges the assumption of clean separability.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The Pareto frontier claim is architecture-dependent to the point of undermining universality.** The paper reports rho=0.41 (Qwen3), 0.22 (Llama4), 0.48 (Gemma4). A rank correlation of 0.22 explains <5% of variance and is barely meaningful. Claiming a \"Pareto frontier\" when the relationship is this noisy on one of three architectures weakens Claim 1 substantially. The paper should acknowledge this is a *tendency* in some architectures rather than a universal structural property.\n\n**W2. CaCLR's consistency loss is inert — this fundamentally undermines Claim 3.** The paper's proposed method contribution is CaCLR (L_BCE + alpha*L_SB-ECE + beta*L_consistency). If sweeping beta over 3 orders of magnitude changes AUROC by <0.3pp, and *removing* it improves by 0.2pp, then CaCLR reduces to L_BCE + L_SB-ECE, which is simply calibration-aware probe training — not a novel cross-lingual alignment method. The \"consistency\" component that was supposed to be the differentiator from linear projection is doing nothing. Furthermore, \"CaCLR never beats linear projection\" on cross-benchmark (Result 7) means the method contribution is essentially negative.\n\n**W3. No confidence intervals or significance tests on the key comparisons.** Effect sizes like Cohen's d=0.18 for probe complexity and the 1.3-1.4pp decomposition degradation are small. Without bootstrap CIs or paired tests across the 19/52 languages, it is impossible to assess whether these effects are reliably non-zero. The MLP transfer penalty of -0.2 to -0.4pp is tiny and may be noise.\n\n**W4. The \"parallel activations\" requirement for CaCLR is underspecified.** The consistency loss requires comparable EN and non-EN activations. How are these obtained? Translated GlobalMMLU items? If so, how is translation quality controlled? Does it require parallel corpora at inference time? This has major practical implications for the \"zero target-language labels\" claim — you still need parallel *inputs*, which may be harder to obtain than 25-100 labeled examples.\n\n**W5. Extended 52-language evaluation (Plan 009) shows 23.5% pass rate and 0% for very-low-resource.** This result significantly undermines the practical value proposition. If the method works only for languages that already have reasonable LLM coverage, the incremental value over simply collecting a few dozen labeled examples per language is questionable.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. What exactly are \"parallel activations\" for L_consistency? Are these from the same MCQA items machine-translated into each language (as in GlobalMMLU)? If so, does CaCLR require access to the evaluation benchmark's translations at training time — creating a form of test-set leakage?\n\nQ2. For the Pareto frontier on Llama 4 Scout (rho=0.22, non-monotonic at layers 20-28), can you provide bootstrap confidence intervals on the rank correlation? With only 12 data points, the sampling uncertainty may be substantial.\n\nQ3. The paper claims \"projecting out difficulty hurts transfer by 1.3-1.4pp\" — but over how many language-layer combinations? Is this statistically significant by a paired test? What is the variance across the 10 languages x 8 layers = 80 conditions?\n\nQ4. Temperature scaling T values (5-6) are extremely high, implying massive overconfidence in the raw probes. Was T optimized on held-out data or the test set? If the latter, ECE numbers are unreliable.\n\nQ5. How sensitive is CaCLR to the choice of alpha? You report beta is inert, but what about alpha (the SB-ECE weight)?\n\n### Minor Issues\n\nM1. Table 5 reports \"Pareto frontier coherence\" across 3 architectures but rho values of 0.41, 0.22, 0.48 do not constitute \"coherence\" in any standard sense. Revise the table title.\n\nM2. The 52-language extension uses only Qwen3 (from context). Cross-architecture validation at this scale would strengthen the claim.\n\nM3. \"Orthogonality persists across 20 languages (mean cosine 0.072)\" — this is interesting but somewhat expected given the finding is architectural, not language-specific. The novelty here is incremental over Miao & Ungar.\n\nM4. The probe complexity finding (MLP +1pp EN, -0.2 to -0.4pp transfer) with Cohen's d=0.18 is a very small effect. Consider whether this table adds value or could move to supplementary.\n\nM5. The paper uses GlobalMMLU, Belebele, and MMLU-ProX — all MCQA. Generalization beyond MCQA remains entirely open.\n\n### Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a timely and important question (does probe-based calibration generalize multilingually?) with thorough experimentation across architectures and languages. The systematic characterization is valuable regardless of whether CaCLR succeeds as a method. However, the method contribution (CaCLR) is effectively negative — the consistency loss is inert and the method never beats linear projection cross-benchmark. The Pareto frontier is weak on 1/3 architectures (rho=0.22). These issues reduce the paper from a strong method+discovery paper to primarily a diagnostic/characterization paper with negative results. For a top venue, the paper needs either (a) a working method that actually shifts the frontier, or (b) explicit reframing as a diagnostic study with the CaCLR negative result as a contribution. Currently it sits uncomfortably between these framings. The breadth of experiments, honest reporting of negative results, and field-level significance of answering \"does this work multilingually?\" push it toward acceptance at a venue like EACL, but the weak method contribution keeps it below the bar for a top ML venue.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":814,"cache_read_input_tokens":73634,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":814,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":67,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DLimNe6JgekSJoEQPJx93R","session_id":"8b4c3866-7c97-4fea-98c8-a02346ba8153","uuid":"f9043fc4-58b9-4935-ad8a-70b190714862"}
